\import{fnip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fnip}
\taxon{Discussion}
\title{Currents held at a fixed voltage}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{A voltage clamp holds the membrane potential at a commanded
value and records the current supplied to keep it there.
\citet{Box 7.1, p. 116}{nicholls2012neuron} describes the
arrangement on the squid axon: two wires run inside the axon, one
reports the membrane potential, and the feedback current equals
the membrane current.
\citet{§1.9, pp. 20–21}{ermentrout2010mathematical} uses the same
arrangement. With the potential held fixed, the capacitive current
vanishes, and a space clamp makes the potential the same at every
point along the axon.}

\p{The potassium and sodium conductances are then the corresponding
currents divided by the difference between the voltage and the
[reversal potential](fnip-0066).
\citet{(1.40), p. 21}{ermentrout2010mathematical} writes
#{g_{\mathrm{K}}=I_{\mathrm{K}}/(V-E_{\mathrm{K}})} and
#{g_{\mathrm{Na}}=I_{\mathrm{Na}}/(V-E_{\mathrm{Na}})}.
Figures 1.8 and 1.9 on pp. 21–22 are a clamp step to #{0} mV and
the conductances at several holding potentials.
\citet{pp. 119–120}{nicholls2012neuron} writes the potassium
conductance as a power #{4} and the sodium conductance as a power
#{3} times a falling exponential.}
